{"attack": true, "case": "The target's core claim that acting on corroboration 'necessarily assumes the uniformity of nature' rests on a false premise: it equates 'preferring' a corroborated theory with 'predicting that A will continue to succeed.' Popper explicitly rejected this equation. His preference for well-corroborated theories is a methodological rule about which theory to subject to further critical testing — the one that survived the most severe tests is the most informative target for the next test — not a prediction of future success or an inference to truth. The target's own countercondition concedes this escape ('If the preference is redefined as a methodological rule for generating new errors rather than a guide to truth'), but the target fails to recognize that Popper's actual published position satisfies exactly that countercondition. Popper defined corroboration in The Logic of Scientific Discovery precisely as a measure of how severely a theory has been tested, not as a probability or predictor of future performance. By assuming that 'preference for future application' necessarily means 'prediction of future success,' the target smuggles in an inductive reading that Popper's framework was specifically constructed to avoid, making the 'smuggling induction back in' charge circular rather than substantive.", "premise": "Popper's preference for corroborated theories is a methodological rule about which theory to test next, not a prediction that the theory will continue to succeed; therefore acting on it does not necessarily assume uniformity of nature.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "f1dab047ca3e77a96e7577b138e4c8a918ce078f4964401550d9ff7ce9442216", "quote": "To prefer Theory A over Theory B for future application is to predict that A will continue to succeed where B might fail. This prediction relies on the assumption that the conditions which allowed A to survive tests will persist."}, {"block": "f1dab047ca3e77a96e7577b138e4c8a918ce078f4964401550d9ff7ce9442216", "quote": "If the preference is redefined as a methodological rule for generating new errors rather than a guide to truth."}], "successor_question": "Did Popper's own published definition of corroboration as a measure of test-severity (not predictive probability) satisfy the target's countercondition, and if so, does the target's 'necessarily' claim collapse?"}